WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.577 line:0%
JASON SILVA: Humanity's struggle
against death

00:00:11.577 --> 00:00:15.748 line:0%
has been our most
enduring fight.

00:00:15.748 --> 00:00:17.350 line:0%
[scream]

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:21.888 line:0%
History has given us one weapon
in this existential battle.

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:25.158 line:0%
We fight back with medicine.

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:26.693 line:0%
Tens of thousands of years ago,

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:30.830 line:0%
our ancestors scavenged
the natural world for remedies.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.566 line:0%
Imagine the incredible leaps
of faith we had to take

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.335 line:0%
in an effort to ease the pain,

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:39.205 line:0%
mend the broken,
and revive the sick.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:41.007 line:0%
Medicine was a dark art,

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:45.111 line:0%
full of false starts
and false gods.

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:47.746 line:0%
But when we struck upon
some form of relief,

00:00:47.746 --> 00:00:52.451 line:0%
it swept like wildfire
through humankind.

00:00:52.451 --> 00:00:55.354 line:0%
We built on every success

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:58.791 line:0%
until medicine became
a form of science,

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:02.528 line:0%
that's when we discovered
the real killers within us.

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:04.830 line:0%
Microscopic armies hidden
within our bodies

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:09.736 line:0%
waging war on our species
for thousands of years.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:12.505 line:0%
Medicine is our great weapon
to fight back

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:16.509 line:0%
against invisible,
unthinkable death.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:23.149 line:0%
[screams]

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:24.784 line:0%
From superstition to science,

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:29.722 line:0%
medicine has become one of
humanity's most powerful tools.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:32.492 line:0%
And though with each
new challenge we may stumble,

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:36.596 line:0%
we find a way to get
stronger and smarter,

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:40.299 line:0%
to beat back fatal diseases
and extend our lifespans.

00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:43.569 line:0%
Even crack open
our genetic code.

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:46.472 line:0%
This is the story of how
our fight against death

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:48.474 line:0%
has determined our fate,

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:51.977 line:0%
how it became the driving force
of our evolution.

00:01:51.977 --> 00:01:57.116 line:0%
How our tireless pursuit
of medicine has made us modern

00:01:57.116 --> 00:02:00.887 line:0%
and even made us superhuman.

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:03.322 line:0%
This is Origins.

00:02:03.322 --> 00:02:12.699 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:13.532 line:0%
It's the greatest

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:15.601 line:0%
adventure story ever told,

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:17.871 line:0%
the story of humankind and

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:20.239 line:0%
how we created the modern world.

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:21.273 line:0%
We're going back in time

00:02:21.273 --> 00:02:23.109 line:0%
to explore key moments,

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:24.310 line:0%
origin moments,

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.344 line:0%
that changed the course

00:02:25.344 --> 00:02:27.213 line:0%
of our shared history.

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:28.247 line:0%
In the beginning,

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:29.848 line:0%
survival was key,

00:02:29.848 --> 00:02:32.017 line:0%
not just from the violent threat
of instant death

00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:33.853 line:0%
posed by predators--no--

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:36.189 line:0%
survival from
all the little things

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:38.324 line:0%
that we take for granted today.

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.993 line:0%
Infection, bacteria, disease.

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:44.130 line:0%
Let's be honest,
anytime before the last century,

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.165 line:0%
the common cold or a bug bite

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:47.600 line:0%
could kill you.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:48.968 line:0%
Medicine is the name that

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:50.436 line:0%
we gave to the magic

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:52.271 line:0%
that allowed us to cheat death

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:55.541 line:0%
again and again and again.

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:59.512 line:0%
And how we learned to fight,
how we have managed to survive.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.347 line:0%
In fact, all the little miracles

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:02.982 line:0%
that medicine brings us today,

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:09.054 line:0%
all of it adds up to a story
about who we are as a species.

00:03:09.054 --> 00:03:11.057 line:0%
These are moments in time,

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:14.560 line:0%
origin moments that offer
irrefutable proof,

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.862 line:0%
medicine has made us modern.

00:03:16.862 --> 00:03:19.331 line:0%
And it's been a key weapon
in our fight for survival

00:03:19.331 --> 00:03:23.069 line:0%
for thousands of years.

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:26.873 line:0%
We didn't know how old
medicine was until 1991.

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:29.241 line:0%
Imagine, you're hiking
through the Eastern Alps

00:03:29.241 --> 00:03:33.111 line:0%
and you spot something
in the snow.

00:03:33.111 --> 00:03:34.747 line:0%
It's an ancient mummy,

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:39.252 line:0%
the oldest and best-preserved
corpse in history.

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:45.791 line:0%
We call him Otzi Man, and
he's more than 5,300 years old.

00:03:45.791 --> 00:03:47.727 line:0%
RAHUL JANDIAL: Otzi Man has
given us an understanding

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:50.162 line:0%
of the origins of medicine.

00:03:50.162 --> 00:03:51.664 line:0%
When we evaluated him,

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:54.233 line:0%
we're able to document
ancient humans' best efforts

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.034 line:0%
at treating the human body.

00:03:56.034 --> 00:03:59.438 line:0%
So, Otzi Man has given us
a glimpse on how he lived,

00:03:59.438 --> 00:04:03.242 line:0%
but we also got an understanding
of how he died.

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:05.678 line:0%
[screams]

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:09.782 line:0%
SILVA: Otzi Man lived in a time
of brutality between humans.

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:11.817 line:0%
Ancient tribes often
fought to the death

00:04:11.817 --> 00:04:16.088 line:0%
over life's most precious
resource, food.

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:18.724 line:0%
[yelling]

00:04:18.724 --> 00:04:34.006 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:35.074 line:0%
[screams]

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:37.710 line:0%
[woman cries]

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:49.154 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:04:49.154 --> 00:04:50.823 line:0%
[yells]

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.327 line:0%
[yells]

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:58.097 line:0%
[coughing]

00:04:58.097 --> 00:05:01.934 line:0%
We know Otzi was a fighter
from the scars on his body,

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:05.671 line:0%
but we also know that he and
his tribe were true believers

00:05:05.671 --> 00:05:09.508 line:0%
in the mysterious
healing powers of nature.

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:10.943 line:0%
THOMAS GOETZ: Medicine was
largely a matter

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.946 line:0%
of whatever was at hand.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:15.748 line:0%
Herbs, tree bark,
those kind of things

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:18.484 line:0%
that may have a healing effect,

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:22.055 line:0%
but it was really kind of
catch-as-catch-can.

00:05:32.831 --> 00:05:36.035 line:0%
JANDIAL: Today, we're inventing
medicines in the laboratory.

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:40.839 line:0%
To do that, we needed thousands
of years of trial and error.

00:05:40.839 --> 00:05:46.512 line:0%
Plants and nature are the
foundation of modern medicine.

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:49.816 line:0%
[thunder]

00:05:56.322 --> 00:05:59.725 line:0%
SILVA: Otzi lived in a world
of unforgiving violence,

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:01.994 line:0%
but Otzi Man and his people
had learned

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:04.930 line:0%
the Earth had remedies
for their pain.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:09.235 line:0%
Natural therapies passed down
from generation to generation,

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:12.772 line:0%
each one improving on the last.

00:06:16.875 --> 00:06:18.678 line:0%
[shouts]

00:06:27.119 --> 00:06:30.156 line:0%
SILVA: Otzi Man put his trust,
his faith,

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:33.559 line:0%
in the healing powers
of the Earth.

00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:38.330 line:0%
But natural medicine
could not protect him

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:41.934 line:0%
from human brutality.

00:06:41.934 --> 00:06:43.536 line:0%
[gasps]

00:06:53.812 --> 00:06:56.248 line:0%
[yells]

00:06:56.248 --> 00:07:03.990 line:0%
[grunting]

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.465 line:0%
[thud]

00:07:13.465 --> 00:07:24.843 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:07:24.843 --> 00:07:26.945 line:0%
JANDIAL: Otzi Man was
found face-down

00:07:26.945 --> 00:07:29.815 line:0%
with a flint arrowhead
in his shoulder.

00:07:29.815 --> 00:07:33.251 line:0%
We think he died of blunt force
trauma to the head.

00:07:33.251 --> 00:07:35.221 line:0%
GOETZ: Otzi Man comes from
a farming community

00:07:35.221 --> 00:07:40.559 line:0%
that was only 5,000 years
into modern man.

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:41.928 line:0%
As a 45-year-old shepherd,

00:07:41.928 --> 00:07:44.096 line:0%
Otzi Man is a living document,
so to speak,

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:47.900 line:0%
of the good and the bad
of those societies.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:50.803 line:0%
In some sense, the agricultural
economy that they were living in

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:53.072 line:0%
was great in terms
of letting them flourish

00:07:53.072 --> 00:07:56.742 line:0%
and have stable lives
and growing families,

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:58.076 line:0%
but then they had
to come to grips

00:07:58.076 --> 00:08:00.312 line:0%
with what are called
mismatched diseases,

00:08:00.312 --> 00:08:04.817 line:0%
such as tooth disease,
dental disease, heart disease.

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:08.020 line:0%
All of those things that
we now think of as human life

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.788 line:0%
and kind of what a lot
of people die from

00:08:09.788 --> 00:08:13.258 line:0%
is attributable to
the civilization we've created.

00:08:13.258 --> 00:08:14.593 line:0%
JANDIAL: All of a sudden,

00:08:14.593 --> 00:08:16.896 line:0%
Otzi and his people were
faced with new diseases

00:08:16.896 --> 00:08:19.331 line:0%
because of the invention
of agriculture.

00:08:19.331 --> 00:08:20.933 line:0%
They needed new remedies.

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:24.269 line:0%
And their only resource is
the natural world around them.

00:08:24.269 --> 00:08:26.738 line:0%
Otzi Man is sort of
a champion survivalist.

00:08:26.738 --> 00:08:28.407 line:0%
It's a testament
to how well he did

00:08:28.407 --> 00:08:30.108 line:0%
that he made it into the 40s,

00:08:30.108 --> 00:08:32.678 line:0%
given how harsh the environment
must have been.

00:08:32.678 --> 00:08:35.081 line:0%
In his stomach,
there were eggs of parasites.

00:08:35.081 --> 00:08:37.849 line:0%
His toes had remnants
of frostbite.

00:08:37.849 --> 00:08:40.186 line:0%
He didn't have the medical
firepower we do now,

00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:42.655 line:0%
but he carried with him
essentially a first-aid kit.

00:08:42.655 --> 00:08:45.991 line:0%
Inside it was a mushroom,
birch polypore,

00:08:45.991 --> 00:08:47.293 line:0%
which could have been
a treatment

00:08:47.293 --> 00:08:49.194 line:0%
for his intestinal parasite.

00:08:49.194 --> 00:08:51.330 line:0%
AMANDA FOREMAN: Everyone thought
that these ancient folk remedies

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.300 line:0%
from thousands of years ago
had nothing left to teach us.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.202 line:0%
But now that these synthetic
drugs have reached a plateau,

00:08:57.202 --> 00:08:58.871 line:0%
scientists are beginning
to realize

00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:00.906 line:0%
that maybe in order
to go forwards,

00:09:00.906 --> 00:09:02.141 line:0%
we need to go backwards

00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:04.977 line:0%
and discover what nature
has to offer us.

00:09:04.977 --> 00:09:07.880 line:0%
JANDIAL: Today, we're inventing
medicines in the laboratory.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.481 line:0%
But the best medicines,

00:09:09.481 --> 00:09:13.719 line:0%
the most effective medicines
were discovered from nature.

00:09:13.719 --> 00:09:15.954 line:0%
We learn from plants and nature,

00:09:15.954 --> 00:09:20.826 line:0%
and then we go to the laboratory
and try to copy them.

00:09:20.826 --> 00:09:22.795 line:0%
SILVA: Our ancestors'
surprisingly modern

00:09:22.795 --> 00:09:24.530 line:0%
and inventive medical treatments

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:25.564 line:0%
have left scientists

00:09:25.564 --> 00:09:26.598 line:0%
scouring the Earth

00:09:26.598 --> 00:09:28.400 line:0%
and reaching back in time

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:30.169 line:0%
for forgotten cures.

00:09:32.304 --> 00:09:35.874 line:0%
[birds squawking]

00:09:35.874 --> 00:09:38.810 line:0%
CASSANDRA QUAVE: About 80%
of the world's population

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:42.581 line:0%
relies on plants as
a major form of medicine.

00:09:42.581 --> 00:09:44.182 line:0%
Looking around in this habitat,

00:09:44.182 --> 00:09:47.419 line:0%
you might see just
some trees and weeds,

00:09:47.419 --> 00:09:49.488 line:0%
and not really think that
there's anything special

00:09:49.488 --> 00:09:50.756 line:0%
about this area.

00:09:50.756 --> 00:09:52.324 line:0%
But what I see is different.

00:09:52.324 --> 00:09:53.926 line:0%
I see medicines.

00:09:53.926 --> 00:09:59.698 line:0%
I see cures from the past
and hopefully, for our future.

00:09:59.698 --> 00:10:04.236 line:0%
The Brazilian pepper tree berry
has a unique mechanism of action

00:10:04.236 --> 00:10:06.839 line:0%
that we've just
started to uncover.

00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:10.075 line:0%
It could be the source
of the next big medicine

00:10:10.075 --> 00:10:13.579 line:0%
in treating really nasty
infections like MRSA

00:10:13.579 --> 00:10:16.849 line:0%
in the future.

00:10:16.849 --> 00:10:20.686 line:0%
Plants make these compounds
to protect themselves.

00:10:20.686 --> 00:10:25.557 line:0%
It works not by killing the
bacteria, but by disarming it.

00:10:25.557 --> 00:10:28.627 line:0%
So, we're harnessing tools
that are already in use

00:10:28.627 --> 00:10:31.931 line:0%
to manage human infections.

00:10:31.931 --> 00:10:35.133 line:0%
We're not looking for
a needle in a haystack.

00:10:35.133 --> 00:10:38.169 line:0%
We know that people have used
these plants as medicines

00:10:38.169 --> 00:10:41.841 line:0%
for hundreds and
hundreds of years.

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:46.712 line:0%
[camera shutter clicks]

00:10:46.712 --> 00:10:48.347 line:0%
BRIAN FAGAN:
Every traditional society

00:10:48.347 --> 00:10:53.786 line:0%
had encyclopedic knowledge
of the medicinal properties

00:10:53.786 --> 00:10:56.789 line:0%
of the plants
in their environment,

00:10:56.789 --> 00:11:01.793 line:0%
because that was the only
medicine they had.

00:11:01.793 --> 00:11:03.428 line:0%
QUAVE: During
the American Civil War,

00:11:03.428 --> 00:11:05.998 line:0%
soldiers would make teas
out of the oak bark

00:11:05.998 --> 00:11:09.935 line:0%
and use it to rinse
their wounds and infections.

00:11:09.935 --> 00:11:12.571 line:0%
Spanish moss has also
been used as a tea

00:11:12.571 --> 00:11:15.207 line:0%
to treat fever and chills.

00:11:15.207 --> 00:11:18.143 line:0%
So, all around us,
we actually are surrounded

00:11:18.143 --> 00:11:24.083 line:0%
by many interesting plants that
could serve as future medicines.

00:11:24.083 --> 00:11:25.451 line:0%
FAGAN: A lot of this
information,

00:11:25.451 --> 00:11:29.087 line:0%
which may seem bizarre
folk medicine to us,

00:11:29.087 --> 00:11:34.427 line:0%
in fact, is pharmaceutically
potentially extremely valuable.

00:11:34.427 --> 00:11:37.663 line:0%
QUAVE: I think there are many,
many more areas of medicine

00:11:37.663 --> 00:11:41.466 line:0%
that can benefit from
improving our understanding

00:11:41.466 --> 00:11:45.371 line:0%
of how these ancient remedies
actually work.

00:11:45.371 --> 00:11:48.273 line:0%
SILVA: These days, we can treat
wounds and cure diseases

00:11:48.273 --> 00:11:52.578 line:0%
that not too long ago would
have meant certain death.

00:11:52.578 --> 00:11:55.214 line:0%
But the medical skills that
made us who we are today

00:11:55.214 --> 00:11:58.484 line:0%
were built by the doctor
who got his start the hard way,

00:11:58.484 --> 00:12:03.022 line:0%
patching up gladiators in
a second century fight club.

00:12:04.857 --> 00:12:07.893 line:0%
[muffled scream]

00:12:22.307 --> 00:12:27.413 line:0%
SILVA: Humanity is cursed by the
knowledge of our own mortality.

00:12:27.413 --> 00:12:30.616 line:0%
The terrifying realization
that life is a fickle flame

00:12:30.616 --> 00:12:34.753 line:0%
that we, and all our loved ones,
are destined for the grave

00:12:34.753 --> 00:12:37.422 line:0%
drives us to find solace.

00:12:37.422 --> 00:12:40.058 line:0%
Religion told us death
is not the end.

00:12:40.058 --> 00:12:44.429 line:0%
The flesh is weak,
but the soul survives.

00:12:44.429 --> 00:12:48.000 line:0%
We could be reborn in infinite
loops of reincarnation

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:51.636 line:0%
or live forever
in the kingdom of heaven.

00:12:51.636 --> 00:12:56.742 line:0%
To our ancestors, religion
offered more than solace.

00:12:56.742 --> 00:12:59.945 line:0%
It offered to cure
our suffering.

00:12:59.945 --> 00:13:01.446 line:0%
For thousands of years,

00:13:01.446 --> 00:13:05.617 line:0%
we invoked the supernatural
in search of remedies.

00:13:05.617 --> 00:13:09.955 line:0%
Medicine and superstition
were woven tightly together.

00:13:09.955 --> 00:13:13.024 line:0%
We turned to divination,
shamans, and white witches

00:13:13.024 --> 00:13:15.661 line:0%
to guide us to long life.

00:13:15.661 --> 00:13:19.664 line:0%
For the ancients,
no remedy was too strange.

00:13:19.664 --> 00:13:25.170 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:27.906 line:0%
The Egyptians mixed spells
and natural cures

00:13:27.906 --> 00:13:30.576 line:0%
into magical remedies.

00:13:30.576 --> 00:13:32.177 line:0%
They pioneered bloodletting,

00:13:32.177 --> 00:13:36.315 line:0%
a cure-all around the world
for 2,000 years.

00:13:36.315 --> 00:13:40.118 line:0%
Even in our deepest desperation,
we pushed on,

00:13:40.118 --> 00:13:44.390 line:0%
slowly uncovering the truth
behind our suffering.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:49.561 line:0%
The pain of losing loved ones
drove us forward.

00:13:49.561 --> 00:13:53.899 line:0%
Over time, the answers began
to reveal themselves.

00:13:53.899 --> 00:13:58.503 line:0%
Superstition was giving
way to science.

00:13:58.503 --> 00:14:06.511 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:14:06.511 --> 00:14:07.846 line:0%
Medical science has come

00:14:07.846 --> 00:14:11.650 line:0%
a long way in the last century.

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:15.220 line:0%
To our ancestors, things like
CT scans and laser surgery

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:17.155 line:0%
might look like magic.

00:14:17.155 --> 00:14:19.725 line:0%
The medical skills that
made us who we are today

00:14:19.725 --> 00:14:21.760 line:0%
were built on a slow and steady

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:23.362 line:0%
accumulation of knowledge

00:14:23.362 --> 00:14:27.132 line:0%
over centuries.

00:14:27.132 --> 00:14:29.968 line:0%
Medical knowledge the world over
came to a head

00:14:29.968 --> 00:14:32.137 line:0%
here in Ancient Greece.

00:14:32.137 --> 00:14:34.039 line:0%
An empire of organized learning

00:14:34.039 --> 00:14:36.175 line:0%
where the ruins of
medical treatment centers

00:14:36.175 --> 00:14:37.743 line:0%
still stand today.

00:14:37.743 --> 00:14:40.379 line:0%
FOREMAN: If you were going to be
sick in the ancient world,

00:14:40.379 --> 00:14:43.449 line:0%
I think you really wanted to be
treated by the ancient Greeks.

00:14:43.449 --> 00:14:46.051 line:0%
For one thing,
they had temples of healing.

00:14:46.051 --> 00:14:47.719 line:0%
These were called asclepeion,

00:14:47.719 --> 00:14:52.558 line:0%
which were basically the world's
first teaching hospitals.

00:14:52.558 --> 00:14:54.259 line:0%
VASSILIS LAMBRINOUDAKIS:
So, we are here

00:14:54.259 --> 00:14:59.265 line:0%
in the most famous sanatorium
of the ancient world...

00:15:05.537 --> 00:15:08.607 line:0%
FOREMAN: But not just a priest
who might say an incantation

00:15:08.607 --> 00:15:11.176 line:0%
or wave a piece of smoky feather
around you

00:15:11.176 --> 00:15:13.812 line:0%
and hope that the spirits
would leave you.

00:15:13.812 --> 00:15:16.281 line:0%
This was based on
trial and error.

00:15:16.281 --> 00:15:20.519 line:0%
Medicine was more
than just magic.

00:15:20.519 --> 00:15:23.222 line:0%
JANDIAL: Patients would visit
this ancient teaching hospital,

00:15:23.222 --> 00:15:25.056 line:0%
they would actually spend
the night and the next day,

00:15:25.056 --> 00:15:26.658 line:0%
report their dreams,

00:15:26.658 --> 00:15:30.029 line:0%
which guided the doctors to
finding the next treatment.

00:15:41.306 --> 00:15:44.042 line:0%
JANDIAL: They actually invented
and developed scalpels.

00:15:44.042 --> 00:15:46.111 line:0%
We can see evidence
of minor surgeries

00:15:46.111 --> 00:15:47.980 line:0%
when we look at the patients
that were treated

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:50.049 line:0%
during that time.

00:16:02.928 --> 00:16:05.097 line:0%
GOETZ: Greek medicine actually
created a structure

00:16:05.097 --> 00:16:06.865 line:0%
for human disease
and human experience

00:16:06.865 --> 00:16:08.734 line:0%
that we still use today.

00:16:08.734 --> 00:16:14.339 line:0%
The Greeks created the ritual
and the process of diagnosis.

00:16:14.339 --> 00:16:15.974 line:0%
JANDIAL: Everything that
we have today

00:16:15.974 --> 00:16:21.013 line:0%
is built on the giant shoulders
of those that came before us.

00:16:22.614 --> 00:16:25.817 line:0%
Every step, every innovation,
is held on to.

00:16:25.817 --> 00:16:30.322 line:0%
That is the scientific method.

00:16:30.322 --> 00:16:31.790 line:0%
But we're continuing to build

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:34.726 line:0%
on the legacy of thousands
and thousands of years

00:16:34.726 --> 00:16:38.230 line:0%
for the fundamental advances
for tomorrow.

00:16:41.533 --> 00:16:43.869 line:0%
SILVA: This trial and error
approach

00:16:43.869 --> 00:16:45.838 line:0%
became the scientific method,

00:16:45.838 --> 00:16:46.872 line:0%
the basis of all

00:16:46.872 --> 00:16:48.173 line:0%
modern medicine.

00:16:48.173 --> 00:16:50.108 line:0%
Observe, experiment,

00:16:50.108 --> 00:16:52.044 line:0%
formulate a hypothesis,

00:16:52.044 --> 00:16:53.879 line:0%
and then test it again and again

00:16:53.879 --> 00:16:55.914 line:0%
until it's proven false or true.

00:16:55.914 --> 00:16:57.348 line:0%
It's a method passed down to us

00:16:57.348 --> 00:17:00.085 line:0%
by the most famous graduate
of the Asklepion,

00:17:00.085 --> 00:17:04.155 line:0%
Galen of Pergamon.

00:17:04.155 --> 00:17:06.692 line:0%
Roughly 200 years after
the birth of Christ,

00:17:06.692 --> 00:17:09.794 line:0%
Galen helped lay the origins
of modern medicine.

00:17:09.794 --> 00:17:11.263 line:0%
[moaning]

00:17:11.263 --> 00:17:14.032 line:0%
GOETZ: Galen was a fight doctor
who actually managed

00:17:14.032 --> 00:17:16.134 line:0%
to write down his best practices

00:17:16.134 --> 00:17:18.336 line:0%
and writing down
what he knew to be true.

00:17:18.336 --> 00:17:21.373 line:0%
He created a corpus that
was not just useful,

00:17:21.373 --> 00:17:25.577 line:0%
but was in fact
revered for centuries.

00:17:25.577 --> 00:17:31.183 line:0%
[crowd shouting]

00:17:33.051 --> 00:17:34.820 line:0%
[growl]

00:17:44.262 --> 00:17:53.705 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:18:00.745 --> 00:18:15.394 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:18:21.032 --> 00:18:27.839 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:18:27.839 --> 00:18:30.209 line:0%
[grunting]

00:19:12.217 --> 00:19:15.354 line:0%
[muffled scream]

00:19:17.456 --> 00:19:21.693 line:0%
[rapid breathing]

00:19:21.693 --> 00:19:25.564 line:0%
[crowd cheering]

00:19:34.206 --> 00:19:35.574 line:0%
SILVA: With each procedure,

00:19:35.574 --> 00:19:39.511 line:0%
Galen was diving into
undiscovered territory.

00:19:39.511 --> 00:19:42.647 line:0%
Lucky for us,
he kept detailed notes

00:19:42.647 --> 00:19:47.186 line:0%
and recorded all his successes
and his failures.

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:54.660 line:0%
[coughs]

00:20:09.841 --> 00:20:11.810 line:0%
[muffled scream]

00:20:11.810 --> 00:20:14.579 line:0%
FOREMAN: The tradition before
Galen was so bad

00:20:14.579 --> 00:20:16.615 line:0%
that you were actually
genuinely likely to die

00:20:16.615 --> 00:20:19.084 line:0%
if you went to see a doctor,

00:20:19.084 --> 00:20:22.387 line:0%
which is why Galen himself
is such an important doctor,

00:20:22.387 --> 00:20:23.888 line:0%
because he wasn't in there

00:20:23.888 --> 00:20:27.692 line:0%
simply to magic something up
and hope for the best.

00:20:27.692 --> 00:20:30.428 line:0%
He had a reason
for treating a patient,

00:20:30.428 --> 00:20:32.998 line:0%
and that patient
he expected to get better.

00:20:32.998 --> 00:20:34.332 line:0%
GOETZ: Starting with Galen,

00:20:34.332 --> 00:20:36.167 line:0%
the history of medicine
is really an effort

00:20:36.167 --> 00:20:38.236 line:0%
to see inside the human body,

00:20:38.236 --> 00:20:41.139 line:0%
to perceive what we can't see.

00:20:41.139 --> 00:20:44.776 line:0%
His discoveries and his books
were incredibly powerful,

00:20:44.776 --> 00:20:46.945 line:0%
because it allowed that
knowledge to be transferred

00:20:46.945 --> 00:20:50.482 line:0%
and disseminated to Arabia,
to Europe eventually,

00:20:50.482 --> 00:20:52.584 line:0%
and became really
the code of medicine

00:20:52.584 --> 00:20:54.152 line:0%
for the next thousand years.

00:20:54.152 --> 00:20:56.921 line:0%
Unfortunately, all of his
autopsies were done on animals,

00:20:56.921 --> 00:20:58.856 line:0%
not on humans.

00:20:58.856 --> 00:21:00.125 line:0%
BILL BRYSON:
For about a thousand years,

00:21:00.125 --> 00:21:02.227 line:0%
Galen colored modern medicine

00:21:02.227 --> 00:21:04.663 line:0%
and the treatments and
people's approaches to it,

00:21:04.663 --> 00:21:06.665 line:0%
but there was an awful lot
that was wrong,

00:21:06.665 --> 00:21:09.734 line:0%
for the principal reason that
Galen couldn't do dissections.

00:21:09.734 --> 00:21:13.772 line:0%
Religion didn't permit
people to do dissections.

00:21:13.772 --> 00:21:16.474 line:0%
Andreas Vesalius,
who was a Belgian doctor,

00:21:16.474 --> 00:21:17.776 line:0%
he came along at a time

00:21:17.776 --> 00:21:21.413 line:0%
when it became possible
to do dissections,

00:21:21.413 --> 00:21:23.048 line:0%
and to actually look
at the human body

00:21:23.048 --> 00:21:25.717 line:0%
and how it was put together.

00:21:25.717 --> 00:21:28.654 line:0%
Leonardo da Vinci, for instance,
with his anatomical drawings,

00:21:28.654 --> 00:21:30.455 line:0%
really began to create
an environment

00:21:30.455 --> 00:21:32.791 line:0%
in which medicine
and human anatomy

00:21:32.791 --> 00:21:34.592 line:0%
was looked at scientifically.

00:21:34.592 --> 00:21:37.061 line:0%
When people actually
studying with,

00:21:37.061 --> 00:21:40.031 line:0%
essentially with an open mind,
how the body works.

00:21:40.031 --> 00:21:42.099 line:0%
It's an immensely complicated
thing that we, even now,

00:21:42.099 --> 00:21:45.003 line:0%
we don't really understand
in a lot of detail.

00:21:45.003 --> 00:21:46.671 line:0%
I mean, nobody really
understands

00:21:46.671 --> 00:21:48.673 line:0%
exactly how the brain
is working, for instance.

00:21:48.673 --> 00:21:50.174 line:0%
But it was the beginning.

00:21:50.174 --> 00:21:54.646 line:0%
It was when we started
to get a handle on all this.

00:21:54.646 --> 00:21:56.748 line:0%
GOETZ: The invention
of the x-ray machine,

00:21:56.748 --> 00:21:58.416 line:0%
the invention of CT scans,

00:21:58.416 --> 00:22:02.721 line:0%
it is all a process of trying
to see what we could not see,

00:22:02.721 --> 00:22:06.024 line:0%
of trying to look
inside our bodies.

00:22:06.024 --> 00:22:08.894 line:0%
JANDIAL: Thousands of years ago,
they only looked at the surface,

00:22:08.894 --> 00:22:12.197 line:0%
and then Galen got us a little
bit deeper inside the skin,

00:22:12.197 --> 00:22:13.598 line:0%
into anatomy.

00:22:13.598 --> 00:22:15.834 line:0%
When I think about
my surgical practice now,

00:22:15.834 --> 00:22:18.570 line:0%
it wouldn't exist
if it were not for Galen.

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:19.871 line:0%
And now with the tools we have,

00:22:19.871 --> 00:22:22.673 line:0%
we're relying on
genetics and DNA

00:22:22.673 --> 00:22:25.476 line:0%
and even atomic interactions
within our body.

00:22:25.476 --> 00:22:27.045 line:0%
So the progress
in modern medicine

00:22:27.045 --> 00:22:30.614 line:0%
is in parallel with the progress
in modern technology.

00:22:30.614 --> 00:22:32.083 line:0%
They go hand in hand.

00:22:32.083 --> 00:22:37.288 line:0%
Better tools means
a better understanding of us.

00:22:37.288 --> 00:22:38.823 line:0%
SILVA: Galen had
a seismic effect

00:22:38.823 --> 00:22:40.726 line:0%
on how your doctor treats you.

00:22:40.726 --> 00:22:43.794 line:0%
Challenging established ideas,
figuring out those missteps,

00:22:43.794 --> 00:22:46.998 line:0%
using science to
prove them wrong.

00:22:46.998 --> 00:22:49.600 line:0%
Our knowledge base is built
on thousands of years

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:52.437 line:0%
of trial and error,

00:22:52.437 --> 00:22:53.538 line:0%
and it all adds up

00:22:53.538 --> 00:22:55.006 line:0%
to who we are now.

00:22:55.006 --> 00:22:55.840 line:0%
We have mastered

00:22:55.840 --> 00:22:57.476 line:0%
the repair of our bodies.

00:22:57.476 --> 00:23:00.612 line:0%
We can come back from what was
once deemed almost certain death

00:23:00.612 --> 00:23:01.713 line:0%
and be remade,

00:23:01.713 --> 00:23:03.481 line:0%
almost good as new.

00:23:03.481 --> 00:23:04.850 line:0%
And yet, one enemy is

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:06.818 line:0%
always lurking, always evolving,

00:23:06.818 --> 00:23:10.288 line:0%
always surprising us with
sickness and deadly disease.

00:23:10.288 --> 00:23:11.322 line:0%
We could have been

00:23:11.322 --> 00:23:12.557 line:0%
wiped out as a species,

00:23:12.557 --> 00:23:14.626 line:0%
if not for a few origin moments

00:23:14.626 --> 00:23:16.027 line:0%
that gave us a fighting chance

00:23:16.027 --> 00:23:20.432 line:0%
against unseen,
almost undetectable killers.

00:23:38.249 --> 00:23:39.584 line:0%
SILVA: The healers of the past

00:23:39.584 --> 00:23:44.122 line:0%
fought blindly against disease
for ages.

00:23:44.122 --> 00:23:49.560 line:0%
But in the 17th century,
their hidden enemy was revealed.

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:51.062 line:0%
Beneath the microscope,

00:23:51.062 --> 00:23:56.101 line:0%
a strange new world
came into focus.

00:23:56.101 --> 00:23:58.436 line:0%
We had our first
tantalizing glimpse

00:23:58.436 --> 00:24:01.840 line:0%
of the hidden realm inside us.

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:05.743 line:0%
Under the lens, flesh became
a latticework of nerves,

00:24:05.743 --> 00:24:07.278 line:0%
capillaries, and blood,

00:24:07.278 --> 00:24:12.150 line:0%
each playing a unique role
in the concert of life.

00:24:13.952 --> 00:24:16.187 line:0%
Everything we thought
we knew about medicine,

00:24:16.187 --> 00:24:20.658 line:0%
about life itself
was cast into doubt.

00:24:20.658 --> 00:24:22.160 line:0%
As we plunged deeper,

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:25.463 line:0%
we came face to face
with an alien universe

00:24:25.463 --> 00:24:29.600 line:0%
filled with miniscule creatures.

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:35.206 line:0%
We had revealed
the realm of bacteria.

00:24:35.206 --> 00:24:38.709 line:0%
Trillions of them live and die
within every one of us.

00:24:38.709 --> 00:24:40.845 line:0%
They are central
to our survival,

00:24:40.845 --> 00:24:42.380 line:0%
yet among them are the killers

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:45.516 line:0%
that have plagued us with
cholera, tuberculosis,

00:24:45.516 --> 00:24:48.586 line:0%
and the Black Death.

00:24:48.586 --> 00:24:50.155 line:0%
Looking deeper still,

00:24:50.155 --> 00:24:54.493 line:0%
we discovered something even
more sinister, the virus.

00:24:56.527 --> 00:25:01.633 line:0%
Secret agents in our system that
hijack the mechanics of biology.

00:25:04.702 --> 00:25:07.506 line:0%
These microbes are
our oldest enemies.

00:25:07.506 --> 00:25:12.944 line:0%
A force we have fought
for centuries.

00:25:12.944 --> 00:25:17.916 line:0%
Over the ages, these tiny armies
have killed billions.

00:25:17.916 --> 00:25:22.254 line:0%
With medicine,
we bring the fight to them.

00:25:29.261 --> 00:25:31.462 line:0%
We live in the Information Age.

00:25:31.462 --> 00:25:32.831 line:0%
An epoch where information can

00:25:32.831 --> 00:25:34.299 line:0%
spread to billions of people

00:25:34.299 --> 00:25:35.433 line:0%
around the globe

00:25:35.433 --> 00:25:36.868 line:0%
instantaneously.

00:25:36.868 --> 00:25:38.569 line:0%
Information that can illuminate,

00:25:38.569 --> 00:25:41.606 line:0%
entertain, and save lives.

00:25:44.209 --> 00:25:47.278 line:0%
In the recent outbreaks of SARS,
measles, and Ebola,

00:25:47.278 --> 00:25:50.448 line:0%
transmission of information
was crucial

00:25:50.448 --> 00:25:52.550 line:0%
to stop disease in its tracks.

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:53.818 line:0%
But imagine finding yourself

00:25:53.818 --> 00:25:55.687 line:0%
stripped of all access
to information

00:25:55.687 --> 00:25:57.289 line:0%
and in the midst

00:25:57.289 --> 00:25:58.656 line:0%
of one of the deadliest plagues

00:25:58.656 --> 00:26:00.191 line:0%
known to humankind

00:26:00.191 --> 00:26:02.226 line:0%
where over the course
of a few centuries,

00:26:02.226 --> 00:26:05.897 line:0%
half of the entire population
of Europe was lost.

00:26:05.897 --> 00:26:08.232 line:0%
This was Europe
in the 16th century.

00:26:08.232 --> 00:26:12.037 line:0%
Black Death stole
up to 200 million lives.

00:26:12.037 --> 00:26:14.338 line:0%
It was an invisible,
relentless tidal wave

00:26:14.338 --> 00:26:18.143 line:0%
washing over the world
for hundreds of years.

00:26:21.612 --> 00:26:23.982 line:0%
[cough]

00:26:23.982 --> 00:26:26.317 line:0%
DIANA PRESTON: Medicine in
the early Middle Ages in Europe

00:26:26.317 --> 00:26:27.652 line:0%
was pretty rudimentary.

00:26:27.652 --> 00:26:30.154 line:0%
A lot of it was to do
with superstition

00:26:30.154 --> 00:26:32.890 line:0%
rather than any sort
of rational treatments.

00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:35.126 line:0%
You might as well have expected
if you had a bad wound

00:26:35.126 --> 00:26:37.061 line:0%
to have someone press
a dead rat to it

00:26:37.061 --> 00:26:39.164 line:0%
in the hopes that
that would cure it.

00:26:39.164 --> 00:26:42.300 line:0%
SILVA: In 1528, one young doctor
was determined

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:45.102 line:0%
to fight the plague
with science.

00:26:45.102 --> 00:26:47.672 line:0%
His name was
Michel de Nostredame,

00:26:47.672 --> 00:26:50.041 line:0%
better known as Nostradamus.

00:26:50.041 --> 00:26:51.275 line:0%
JANDIAL: When we think
of Nostradamus,

00:26:51.275 --> 00:26:53.144 line:0%
we think about
all his prophecies,

00:26:53.144 --> 00:26:56.347 line:0%
but actually he was a plague
doctor taking care of the sick,

00:26:56.347 --> 00:26:58.082 line:0%
as a young clinician.

00:26:58.082 --> 00:27:00.317 line:0%
[coughing]

00:27:00.317 --> 00:27:02.587 line:0%
We know now the Black Death
is an infection,

00:27:02.587 --> 00:27:04.189 line:0%
but back then they didn't know,

00:27:04.189 --> 00:27:07.825 line:0%
so they blamed everything
from God to foreigners

00:27:07.825 --> 00:27:10.461 line:0%
and even earthquakes.

00:27:10.461 --> 00:27:15.533 line:0%
[bell tolling]

00:27:15.533 --> 00:27:26.444 line:0%
[coughing]

00:27:26.444 --> 00:27:29.280 line:0%
[loud bang]

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:36.988 line:0%
[footsteps]

00:27:36.988 --> 00:27:39.924 line:0%
[knock on door, woman screams]

00:28:10.521 --> 00:28:42.787 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:28:55.933 --> 00:28:58.870 line:0%
[screams]

00:28:58.870 --> 00:29:01.505 line:0%
[door slams]

00:29:01.505 --> 00:29:52.690 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:55.960 line:0%
[crying]

00:29:55.960 --> 00:30:01.366 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:30:01.366 --> 00:30:03.734 line:0%
[horse whinnies]

00:30:03.734 --> 00:30:35.834 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:31:01.492 --> 00:31:03.561 line:0%
JANDIAL: Nostradamus'
contribution to the Black Death

00:31:03.561 --> 00:31:05.963 line:0%
was getting people
to boil water,

00:31:05.963 --> 00:31:08.132 line:0%
was getting people to bury
their dead.

00:31:08.132 --> 00:31:11.101 line:0%
This led to a whole new
awareness for sanitation

00:31:11.101 --> 00:31:13.337 line:0%
and its link with health.

00:31:13.337 --> 00:31:14.772 line:0%
GOETZ: The invention
of sanitation,

00:31:14.772 --> 00:31:17.942 line:0%
the recognition that
we needed to have sewers,

00:31:17.942 --> 00:31:20.211 line:0%
that we needed
to take human waste

00:31:20.211 --> 00:31:25.416 line:0%
and move it as far as possible
from human settlements.

00:31:25.416 --> 00:31:28.586 line:0%
That was in many ways
the greatest invention

00:31:28.586 --> 00:31:31.822 line:0%
one would say of medicine.

00:31:31.822 --> 00:31:35.293 line:0%
FOREMAN: What separates
town dwellers from nomads,

00:31:35.293 --> 00:31:37.294 line:0%
people who live
in nomadic societies

00:31:37.294 --> 00:31:39.196 line:0%
can just get up and walk away.

00:31:39.196 --> 00:31:41.632 line:0%
Town dwellers can't.

00:31:41.632 --> 00:31:44.636 line:0%
And why this is important
is because town dwellers

00:31:44.636 --> 00:31:47.772 line:0%
have to deal with
their own refuse.

00:31:47.772 --> 00:31:50.173 line:0%
That required organization.

00:31:50.173 --> 00:31:51.976 line:0%
It required politics.

00:31:51.976 --> 00:31:54.645 line:0%
It required a system
of sanitation.

00:31:54.645 --> 00:31:59.817 line:0%
You're seeing the beginnings of
what we now call civic society.

00:31:59.817 --> 00:32:02.419 line:0%
GOETZ: If you want to just count
the number of lives saved,

00:32:02.419 --> 00:32:05.490 line:0%
sanitation is perhaps
one of the greatest discoveries

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:08.292 line:0%
we've ever made.

00:32:08.292 --> 00:32:10.227 line:0%
SILVA: The plague pandemics
are a prime example

00:32:10.227 --> 00:32:11.696 line:0%
of how lack of knowledge

00:32:11.696 --> 00:32:14.732 line:0%
and the inability to spread
accurate information

00:32:14.732 --> 00:32:16.701 line:0%
can lead to false assumptions.

00:32:16.701 --> 00:32:18.669 line:0%
At every turn in our existence,

00:32:18.669 --> 00:32:20.037 line:0%
the quest for truth

00:32:20.037 --> 00:32:21.239 line:0%
by examination

00:32:21.239 --> 00:32:23.641 line:0%
has provided the key
to our survival

00:32:23.641 --> 00:32:25.342 line:0%
in the face of disaster.

00:32:25.342 --> 00:32:27.978 line:0%
When we started to get
a clearer picture of our body

00:32:27.978 --> 00:32:29.280 line:0%
and how our system works,

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:31.449 line:0%
we discovered we are not alone.

00:32:31.449 --> 00:32:33.918 line:0%
We now know we carry
life-forms within us,

00:32:33.918 --> 00:32:38.622 line:0%
trillions upon trillions, many
of them good, some of them bad.

00:32:38.622 --> 00:32:39.657 line:0%
The first discovery

00:32:39.657 --> 00:32:40.858 line:0%
of these microbes

00:32:40.858 --> 00:32:42.393 line:0%
opened a whole new battlefront

00:32:42.393 --> 00:32:44.361 line:0%
in the war against disease.

00:32:44.361 --> 00:32:46.163 line:0%
It's a fight that
continues today,

00:32:46.163 --> 00:32:47.565 line:0%
attacking the origins

00:32:47.565 --> 00:32:48.599 line:0%
of illness itself,

00:32:48.599 --> 00:32:52.203 line:0%
beating back
the specter of death!

00:33:11.255 --> 00:33:12.290 line:0%
SILVA: We know today that our

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:14.658 line:0%
macroscopic world is controlled

00:33:14.658 --> 00:33:16.660 line:0%
by microscopic beings.

00:33:16.660 --> 00:33:17.895 line:0%
Most of them help us,

00:33:17.895 --> 00:33:20.164 line:0%
but some microbes are killers.

00:33:20.164 --> 00:33:21.198 line:0%
Medical technology

00:33:21.198 --> 00:33:22.767 line:0%
is now so powerful

00:33:22.767 --> 00:33:26.737 line:0%
we can see them in fine detail.

00:33:26.737 --> 00:33:28.106 line:0%
But in the 19th century,

00:33:28.106 --> 00:33:30.474 line:0%
doctors had no idea
what caused epidemics

00:33:30.474 --> 00:33:32.142 line:0%
of cholera, yellow fever,

00:33:32.142 --> 00:33:34.077 line:0%
influenza, measles,

00:33:34.077 --> 00:33:35.546 line:0%
diseases that ravaged

00:33:35.546 --> 00:33:39.650 line:0%
the most sophisticated
modern societies on Earth.

00:33:39.650 --> 00:33:41.452 line:0%
BRYSON: A lot of
the very finest minds

00:33:41.452 --> 00:33:44.555 line:0%
put themselves to the challenge
of understanding the human body

00:33:44.555 --> 00:33:46.490 line:0%
and illness and how they work.

00:33:46.490 --> 00:33:48.158 line:0%
But the problem is
that the human body

00:33:48.158 --> 00:33:49.727 line:0%
is a very complex thing,

00:33:49.727 --> 00:33:52.530 line:0%
and they didn't understand
one of the basic mechanisms,

00:33:52.530 --> 00:33:54.932 line:0%
which is that a lot
of what determines

00:33:54.932 --> 00:34:01.339 line:0%
how healthy we are or not
is invisible, it's microbial.

00:34:01.339 --> 00:34:02.873 line:0%
SILVA: Imagine what
it must have been like

00:34:02.873 --> 00:34:06.344 line:0%
to look into a microscope
and see for the first time

00:34:06.344 --> 00:34:08.746 line:0%
organisms invisible to the eye,

00:34:08.746 --> 00:34:11.782 line:0%
operating in our blood,
within our bodies.

00:34:11.782 --> 00:34:13.617 line:0%
But how would you know
if you were looking at

00:34:13.617 --> 00:34:18.889 line:0%
the product of a disease
or its cause?

00:34:18.889 --> 00:34:21.992 line:0%
This was the central question
facing a young doctor

00:34:21.992 --> 00:34:26.830 line:0%
working in the outskirts
of the German empire in 1875.

00:34:26.830 --> 00:34:29.533 line:0%
His name was Robert Koch.

00:34:29.533 --> 00:34:40.445 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:35:13.110 --> 00:35:15.645 line:0%
GOETZ: Robert Koch was a country
doctor in a small town

00:35:15.645 --> 00:35:17.915 line:0%
that was filled
with sheep farmers

00:35:17.915 --> 00:35:19.516 line:0%
and had an outbreak of anthrax.

00:35:19.516 --> 00:35:23.154 line:0%
They asked him to find out what
was killing the local sheep.

00:35:23.154 --> 00:35:27.358 line:0%
So Koch started
a series of experiments.

00:35:27.358 --> 00:35:30.061 line:0%
Robert Koch believed
the germ theory of disease,

00:35:30.061 --> 00:35:33.564 line:0%
that disease could spread
by means of microbes,

00:35:33.564 --> 00:35:35.899 line:0%
these things we observed
under microscopes.

00:35:35.899 --> 00:35:40.904 line:0%
People had observed bacteria
for many hundreds of years.

00:35:40.904 --> 00:35:42.473 line:0%
There was no way to prove

00:35:42.473 --> 00:35:45.242 line:0%
that that had anything to do
with causing the disease.

00:35:45.242 --> 00:35:47.578 line:0%
In fact, it was thought
that they resulted

00:35:47.578 --> 00:35:52.616 line:0%
as a consequence of the disease.

00:35:52.616 --> 00:35:53.918 line:0%
[gasps]

00:36:19.176 --> 00:36:23.346 line:0%
GOETZ: So Koch took the blood
of the local sheep,

00:36:23.346 --> 00:36:28.518 line:0%
and he moved it
to another animal.

00:36:28.518 --> 00:36:31.154 line:0%
And that animal died.

00:36:31.154 --> 00:36:37.361 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:36:37.361 --> 00:36:41.832 line:0%
[ticking]

00:36:41.832 --> 00:36:46.370 line:0%
He saw bacteria in the blood
that had an outbreak of anthrax.

00:37:01.185 --> 00:37:04.521 line:0%
GOETZ: After he had reproduced
the disease in 20 or 30 animals,

00:37:04.521 --> 00:37:09.459 line:0%
and in each case seen the blood
go from pure and clean

00:37:09.459 --> 00:37:11.795 line:0%
to infested
with these pathogens,

00:37:11.795 --> 00:37:15.499 line:0%
Koch had actually created
a chain of evidence

00:37:15.499 --> 00:37:19.470 line:0%
that had identified that
there was in fact one bacteria,

00:37:19.470 --> 00:37:23.674 line:0%
one pathogen that
caused this disease.

00:37:23.674 --> 00:37:24.975 line:0%
The germ theory of disease

00:37:24.975 --> 00:37:28.512 line:0%
became a true breakthrough
of medicine.

00:37:32.816 --> 00:37:34.852 line:0%
BRYSON: Right up until
the time of Koch,

00:37:34.852 --> 00:37:39.023 line:0%
a common notion was that
maladies were caused by bad air.

00:37:39.023 --> 00:37:40.658 line:0%
Miasmas, as they were known.

00:37:40.658 --> 00:37:43.493 line:0%
I mean, malaria means 'bad air.'

00:37:43.493 --> 00:37:45.062 line:0%
It just seems
so counterintuitive,

00:37:45.062 --> 00:37:47.765 line:0%
the idea that something
small and invisible

00:37:47.765 --> 00:37:51.468 line:0%
could make you ill
and even kill you.

00:37:51.468 --> 00:37:53.604 line:0%
GOETZ: Eventually,
Robert Koch started to make

00:37:53.604 --> 00:37:56.040 line:0%
this amazing pattern
of discoveries.

00:37:56.040 --> 00:37:59.142 line:0%
Typhus, anthrax, tuberculosis.

00:37:59.142 --> 00:38:01.745 line:0%
All these diseases that caused
millions of deaths a year

00:38:01.745 --> 00:38:04.048 line:0%
were suddenly catalogued
and identified

00:38:04.048 --> 00:38:06.683 line:0%
as being caused by bacteria.

00:38:06.683 --> 00:38:09.319 line:0%
And so Robert Koch signs up
to give a lecture

00:38:09.319 --> 00:38:12.089 line:0%
in a library in Berlin.

00:38:12.089 --> 00:38:14.725 line:0%
The doctors in the room
were there to challenge Koch.

00:38:14.725 --> 00:38:18.762 line:0%
They had staked their careers
on arguing against germs.

00:38:18.762 --> 00:38:24.168 line:0%
And he finished his lecture, and
the room was completely silent.

00:38:24.168 --> 00:38:26.470 line:0%
Paul Ehrlich, another famous
scientist who was in the room,

00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:30.274 line:0%
said he knew that he had
witnessed a pivotal moment

00:38:30.274 --> 00:38:31.542 line:0%
in medical history.

00:38:31.542 --> 00:38:34.344 line:0%
And the world had
suddenly changed.

00:38:34.344 --> 00:38:37.814 line:0%
FOREMAN: The ability to identify
and prevent infectious diseases

00:38:37.814 --> 00:38:41.885 line:0%
is the key to almost all
of modern medical science.

00:38:41.885 --> 00:38:46.023 line:0%
Without it, humanity can die
in a matter of months.

00:38:46.023 --> 00:38:47.624 line:0%
GOETZ: The germ theory
of disease

00:38:47.624 --> 00:38:50.461 line:0%
led us to the creation
of vaccines,

00:38:50.461 --> 00:38:52.796 line:0%
and that was a pivotal moment.

00:38:52.796 --> 00:38:55.032 line:0%
FOREMAN: We haven't yet
conquered death, obviously,

00:38:55.032 --> 00:38:57.701 line:0%
but one of the things
that we have conquered

00:38:57.701 --> 00:38:59.870 line:0%
thanks to vaccinations

00:38:59.870 --> 00:39:06.176 line:0%
is the insane infant mortality
rate that used to exist.

00:39:06.176 --> 00:39:09.646 line:0%
A hundred years ago, families
had to be of, you know,

00:39:09.646 --> 00:39:11.381 line:0%
three, four, five,
seven children,

00:39:11.381 --> 00:39:12.649 line:0%
because at least half of them

00:39:12.649 --> 00:39:14.685 line:0%
weren't going to make it
to adulthood.

00:39:14.685 --> 00:39:18.655 line:0%
And of course it prevented women
from having careers,

00:39:18.655 --> 00:39:20.658 line:0%
having lives outside
of the family.

00:39:20.658 --> 00:39:22.259 line:0%
But thanks to vaccinations,

00:39:22.259 --> 00:39:25.395 line:0%
we can be pretty sure that
the children we give birth to

00:39:25.395 --> 00:39:26.997 line:0%
are going to make it.

00:39:26.997 --> 00:39:31.469 line:0%
Suddenly, you now only need
to have one or two children.

00:39:31.469 --> 00:39:37.808 line:0%
Women are no longer just baby
machines, they are human beings.

00:39:37.808 --> 00:39:40.077 line:0%
And so you have
the rise of feminism.

00:39:40.077 --> 00:39:43.047 line:0%
You get the rise
of the middle classes.

00:39:43.047 --> 00:39:47.518 line:0%
So in fact, there's this huge
social revolution

00:39:47.518 --> 00:39:50.788 line:0%
that comes out of this
medical revolution.

00:39:54.892 --> 00:39:57.261 line:0%
SILVA: The collective wisdom
of all of humankind

00:39:57.261 --> 00:39:58.528 line:0%
led to the medical advancements

00:39:58.528 --> 00:40:00.197 line:0%
that made us modern.

00:40:00.197 --> 00:40:04.034 line:0%
We're attacking the things that
harm us on a microscopic level.

00:40:04.034 --> 00:40:07.637 line:0%
We're finding new ways
of preventing disease every day.

00:40:07.637 --> 00:40:12.676 line:0%
The question is,
how far can we go?

00:40:12.676 --> 00:40:15.579 line:0%
JANDIAL: What seems to be
fantasy and sci-fi

00:40:15.579 --> 00:40:17.881 line:0%
in the world of health
and the human body

00:40:17.881 --> 00:40:21.085 line:0%
is actually just right
around the corner.

00:40:21.085 --> 00:40:25.455 line:0%
Imagine having your own personal
catalog of replacement tissue

00:40:25.455 --> 00:40:27.224 line:0%
sitting on a shelf.

00:40:27.224 --> 00:40:32.530 line:0%
We can tweak a skin cell
and turn it into a brain cell

00:40:32.530 --> 00:40:34.632 line:0%
and rebuild your body parts.

00:40:34.632 --> 00:40:37.334 line:0%
That's the horizon,
and it's not far away.

00:40:37.334 --> 00:40:38.735 line:0%
It's not 50 years away.

00:40:38.735 --> 00:40:41.138 line:0%
It's going on right now.

00:40:43.440 --> 00:40:45.575 line:0%
DEVIN LEAKE:
This lab is to genetics

00:40:45.575 --> 00:40:49.946 line:0%
what the printing press
was to written word.

00:40:49.946 --> 00:40:53.183 line:0%
ELIZABETH NICKERSON: Here in
this lab, we synthesize DNA,

00:40:53.183 --> 00:40:56.352 line:0%
so a lot of people have heard
about the Human Genome Project

00:40:56.352 --> 00:40:58.088 line:0%
where DNA was decoded,

00:40:58.088 --> 00:41:02.926 line:0%
but here what we're doing is
actually building pieces of DNA.

00:41:02.926 --> 00:41:04.728 line:0%
The DNA code in every cell

00:41:04.728 --> 00:41:06.564 line:0%
is not only a source
of information,

00:41:06.564 --> 00:41:09.132 line:0%
for example, your eye color,
your hair color, your height,

00:41:09.132 --> 00:41:10.935 line:0%
but it's also
a source of control

00:41:10.935 --> 00:41:12.936 line:0%
of different processes
in your body,

00:41:12.936 --> 00:41:16.207 line:0%
things like regenerating cells
at different speeds

00:41:16.207 --> 00:41:18.942 line:0%
in different parts of your body.

00:41:18.942 --> 00:41:22.079 line:0%
LEAKE: It's an incredible,
incredible technology.

00:41:22.079 --> 00:41:26.517 line:0%
Our overarching goal is
making DNA ubiquitous,

00:41:26.517 --> 00:41:31.255 line:0%
to make it available
to everyone.

00:41:31.255 --> 00:41:32.923 line:0%
KEVIN MUNNELLY: The things that
we're working on right now

00:41:32.923 --> 00:41:35.025 line:0%
we know can be game changing,

00:41:35.025 --> 00:41:41.464 line:0%
better, more specific drugs,
personalized drugs.

00:41:41.464 --> 00:41:44.735 line:0%
NICKERSON: We're enabling a lot
of different types of projects.

00:41:44.735 --> 00:41:46.770 line:0%
But I always like to think
about the people

00:41:46.770 --> 00:41:48.205 line:0%
that are studying cancer.

00:41:48.205 --> 00:41:51.908 line:0%
There's been a lot of cancer
in my family.

00:41:51.908 --> 00:41:55.145 line:0%
Cancer is a very
dynamic disease.

00:41:55.145 --> 00:41:58.214 line:0%
But if you can
accelerate research,

00:41:58.214 --> 00:42:02.552 line:0%
then you can actually create
a designer therapy

00:42:02.552 --> 00:42:04.288 line:0%
for a specific tumor.

00:42:04.288 --> 00:42:07.024 line:0%
And the faster you can do that,
the earlier you can intervene,

00:42:07.024 --> 00:42:09.827 line:0%
and that's everything in cancer.

00:42:11.795 --> 00:42:13.330 line:0%
MUNNELLY: In the world
of synthetic biology,

00:42:13.330 --> 00:42:15.732 line:0%
it's either going to be
the thing that saves humanity

00:42:15.732 --> 00:42:18.435 line:0%
or the thing that
kills humanity.

00:42:18.435 --> 00:42:20.003 line:0%
So we're part of an organization

00:42:20.003 --> 00:42:22.139 line:0%
that self-polices
in the industry.

00:42:22.139 --> 00:42:25.809 line:0%
We have different protocols
to ensure that none of us

00:42:25.809 --> 00:42:30.247 line:0%
will design and build something
harmful to humanity.

00:42:30.247 --> 00:42:32.115 line:0%
LEAKE: I think as we think
about medicine,

00:42:32.115 --> 00:42:34.985 line:0%
this is the next revolution
in medicine.

00:42:34.985 --> 00:42:39.089 line:0%
This is the ability to really
create personalized medicine.

00:42:39.089 --> 00:42:43.894 line:0%
It's an opportunity
to help humankind.

00:42:43.894 --> 00:42:47.063 line:0%
SILVA: With the discovery of DNA
and the mechanisms of genetics,

00:42:47.063 --> 00:42:49.399 line:0%
we have discovered
the language of life.

00:42:49.399 --> 00:42:52.603 line:0%
We have realized that we are
linguistic all the way down.

00:42:52.603 --> 00:42:55.238 line:0%
DNA is code,
and we are diving deeper

00:42:55.238 --> 00:42:57.574 line:0%
into what makes us
uniquely human.

00:42:57.574 --> 00:42:59.376 line:0%
We are repairing flaws

00:42:59.376 --> 00:43:00.511 line:0%
that have built up

00:43:00.511 --> 00:43:02.312 line:0%
over millennia of evolution,

00:43:02.312 --> 00:43:04.214 line:0%
so we can chase off death

00:43:04.214 --> 00:43:09.486 line:0%
and thrive in this modern world
today and beyond tomorrow.

00:43:09.486 --> 00:43:10.820 line:0%
As Alan Harrington once wrote,

00:43:10.820 --> 00:43:12.890 line:0%
'Any philosophy
that accepts death

00:43:12.890 --> 00:43:16.760 line:0%
must itself be considered dead,
its questions meaningless,

00:43:16.760 --> 00:43:19.964 line:0%
its consolations worn out.'

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:26.536 line:0%
Across time,
the advancement of medicine

00:43:26.536 --> 00:43:29.640 line:0%
has done more than save lives.

00:43:29.640 --> 00:43:31.342 line:0%
It has given us
a new understanding

00:43:31.342 --> 00:43:35.879 line:0%
of ourselves and our world.

00:43:35.879 --> 00:43:37.748 line:0%
We have grasped
the inner workings

00:43:37.748 --> 00:43:41.251 line:0%
of our bodies and our minds.

00:43:41.251 --> 00:43:45.288 line:0%
We have gazed upon the very
building blocks of life,

00:43:45.288 --> 00:43:50.828 line:0%
the clockwork of biology
that makes us tick.

00:43:50.828 --> 00:43:54.931 line:0%
We have even discovered
an entirely new way of thinking.

00:43:54.931 --> 00:43:58.001 line:0%
Through medicine, powerful ideas
about logic and reason

00:43:58.001 --> 00:44:02.106 line:0%
have taken root and
blossomed into science.

00:44:05.008 --> 00:44:08.278 line:0%
Even when the unknown
outweighed the known,

00:44:08.278 --> 00:44:11.315 line:0%
even as we fought
an invisible enemy,

00:44:11.315 --> 00:44:14.818 line:0%
we refused to accept
the inevitable.

00:44:14.818 --> 00:44:19.322 line:0%
With medicine,
we have seized our own fate.

00:44:19.322 --> 00:44:22.426 line:0%
We owe it all to the visionaries
of medicine,

00:44:22.426 --> 00:44:25.963 line:0%
the healers and thinkers
who paved the way.

00:44:25.963 --> 00:44:27.664 line:0%
We have come far.

00:44:27.664 --> 00:44:30.433 line:0%
Our species is on the verge
of medical revolutions

00:44:30.433 --> 00:44:34.504 line:0%
that will change what
it means to be human.

00:44:34.504 --> 00:44:36.206 line:0%
As we turn to face the future,

00:44:36.206 --> 00:44:40.411 line:0%
we fight death with more power
than ever before,

00:44:40.411 --> 00:44:44.214 line:0%
standing on the shoulders
of giants.

00:44:44.214 --> 00:44:47.617 line:0%
We are ready to face
the unknown.

00:44:47.617 --> 00:45:02.499 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:45:02.499 --> 00:45:06.704 line:0%
We will fight until we see
the end of disease...

00:45:08.939 --> 00:45:13.476 line:0%
...and just maybe,
the end of death.

00:45:13.476 --> 00:45:19.783 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:45:19.783 --> 00:45:29.860 line:0%
[bell tolling]

